cff-version: 1.2.0
# This file describes the SOFTWARE. preferred-citation below is the paper, which is what a
# reader of the results should cite; the two carry the same title on purpose, because the
# repository has no separate identity from the study it produced.
title: >-
  Apparent sequence-model contribution depends strongly on
  negative-set construction: analysis code and released evidence
message: >-
  If you use this software or its released evidence, please cite
  the preprint. No DOI exists yet: the archival deposit is minted
  from a tagged release. Add a doi: key here when it exists.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Nirmalkumar
    family-names: Thirupallikrishnan Kesavan
    email: thirupallikrishnan.n@northeastern.edu
    affiliation: Northeastern University
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0002-9977-1739'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/NirmalKumar31/rbp-protocol-calibration'
abstract: >-
  A calibration study across 94 paired ENCODE eCLIP datasets.
  Holding the model class, the peak set, the chromosome-blocked
  folds and the estimator fixed, refitting model and baseline
  within each protocol, and varying how negative windows
  are constructed moves a model's measured nested contribution
  4.84-fold under the cross-fitted estimator the paper recommends
  and reports as primary, and 5.42-fold under the two-stage one
  this literature uses, while its apparent AUROC moves the
  opposite way.
  The two-stage estimator also returns a positive increment for a
  model whose information the composition baseline already
  contains, where the true contribution is zero by construction;
  cross-fitting the covariate removes at least 95 percent of it.
keywords:
  - RNA-binding proteins
  - eCLIP
  - negative sets
  - benchmark calibration
  - sequence models
# MIT ALONE, DELIBERATELY, AND NOT BECAUSE THE REPOSITORY IS SINGLE-LICENSED. Code is MIT;
# `results/` and `data/evidence/` are CC BY 4.0, which their own LICENSE files record and
# which the archival deposit preserves. The CFF schema does permit a list
# here, but it defines the relationship as OR: "When there are multiple licenses, it is assumed
# their relationship is OR, not AND". Ours is AND, one licence per part of the tree, so
# `[MIT, CC-BY-4.0]` would tell a reader they may take the code under CC BY or the evidence
# under MIT. That is a broader grant than is offered. This record is `type: software`, so it
# names the software licence and points at the others rather than misstating the relationship.
license: MIT
# 1.0.0 at the release. A version number in a citation file is a claim that something
# immutable is named by it, and from this commit something is: tag v1.0.0 and the Zenodo
# version DOI that resolves to it. The tag, this file and pyproject.toml must agree, and
# tests/unit/test_no_stale_contracts.py fails the build if they do not.
version: 1.0.0

preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: >-
    Apparent sequence-model contribution depends strongly on
    negative-set construction: a calibration across 94 ENCODE eCLIP
    datasets
  authors:
    - given-names: Nirmalkumar
      family-names: Thirupallikrishnan Kesavan
      email: thirupallikrishnan.n@northeastern.edu
      affiliation: Northeastern University
      orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0002-9977-1739'
  year: 2026
